Progress status for this period: on_track. This was computed from strengthIndex by a fixed rule, so treat it as settled and use progressiveOverload.notes to explain it with the specific numbers. The rule: regressing if any muscle group's index dropped 3+ points, otherwise on_track if any rose 3+ points, otherwise stalling; changes are measured against previousIndex, or against 100 (the user's first recorded level) when previousIndex is null.

Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. Only make physiological claims that are well established — for example, do not claim that training one muscle group raises hormone levels enough to drive muscle growth elsewhere. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Your back is getting stronger — keep building on this momentum.",
  "summary": "Solid week overall. With 12 sets and over 6,000 kg of tonnage directed at your back, you are clearly prioritizing the right muscle group for your goal of building a bigger back. The back strength index jumped from 106 to 110, which is a meaningful gain in a single period and confirms that your effort is translating into real progress. Total volume across all muscle groups was 28 sets at 72 kg bodyweight, which is a reasonable and sustainable workload for a 28-year-old male at a healthy BMI of 23.5. Recovery appears manageable at this level. Keep the current approach as your foundation and look for small opportunities to sharpen balance and variety.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "notes": "The back strength index rose from 106 to 110 this period, a gain of 4 points, which is the clearest sign of progressive overload in the data and the primary reason this period is classified as on_track. Every other muscle group held steady with no change in their index: biceps stayed at 104, chest at 100, shoulders at 101, and legs at 103. None of those groups regressed, which is good, but none made forward progress either. The gains are happening exactly where you want them given the goal of building a bigger back, and the 55.6% volume split directed to the back supports that result.",
    "status": "on_track"
  },
  "muscleImbalances": [
    {
      "muscleGroup": "chest",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Chest received only 2 sets this period, accounting for 8.9% of total volume. While the back-focused goal justifies reduced chest work, 2 sets is minimal and may be insufficient to maintain chest strength over time, especially as the back continues to grow and pulling strength increases relative to pushing strength."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "shoulders",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Shoulders received only 2 sets at 5.2% of total volume, the lowest share of any group. Shoulders support both pressing and pulling movements, and chronically low shoulder volume alongside heavy back training could create a push-pull imbalance over a longer training cycle."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": false,
  "deloadReason": null,
  "actionItems": [
    "Continue prioritizing back volume at or near 12 sets per period, as this directly supports your goal and produced a 4-point strength index gain this week.",
    "Add at least 1 to 2 more sets for chest and shoulders next period to prevent a push-pull imbalance from developing as your back gets progressively stronger.",
    "Aim to nudge up the load or reps on at least one key back exercise to keep the strength index rising, since a jump from 106 to 110 sets a high bar to continue matching.",
    "Monitor how your biceps respond over the next period — at 6 sets and a flat index of 104, they are doing enough volume to hold steady, but since they are heavily involved in back pulling movements, keeping an eye on fatigue there is worthwhile."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Back strength index surged from 106 to 110 with 12 sets and 6,000 kg tonnage; all other groups held steady; mild imbalance concern with chest and shoulders each receiving only 2 sets."
}
